Title: Diagnosis of test `testEquals` failure in `StringUtilsEqualsIndexOfTest`

Details: 
The failed test case is `testEquals` in the class `StringUtilsEqualsIndexOfTest`. The failure is triggered by the line:
```java
assertTrue(StringUtils.equals(fooCs, (CharSequence) new StringBuilder(FOO)));
```
which expects the `equals` method of `StringUtils` to return `true` when comparing a `CharSequence` `fooCs` (which is `FOO`) with a `CharSequence` constructed as a `StringBuilder` containing the same string `FOO`.

The `StringUtils.equals(CharSequence, CharSequence)` method implementation is as follows:
```java
public static boolean equals(CharSequence cs1, CharSequence cs2) {
    if (cs1 == cs2) {
        return true;
    }
    if (cs1 == null || cs2 == null) {
        return false;
    }
    return cs1.equals(cs2);
}
```
From this, if neither of the CharSequences is null and they are not the same instance (`cs1 == cs2` is false), it falls back to calling `cs1.equals(cs2)`.

The likely cause of failure is that the `equals` method on the first `CharSequence` (which is presumably a `String`) is invoked on a `String` object, compared with the second `CharSequence` which is a `StringBuilder`. Since `String.equals` method does not consider a `StringBuilder` equal even if it contains the same characters, the result is `false`. This is because `String.equals` only returns true if the other object is also a `String` with the same character sequence, not a generic `CharSequence` with the same content.

Hence, the root cause is that the current implementation of `StringUtils.equals` relies on the `equals` implementation of the first `CharSequence` which assumes the other object must be the same type, and thus it does not correctly compare content sequences of different `CharSequence` implementations.

To fix this, `StringUtils.equals` needs to compare the sequences by their character content rather than relying on `cs1.equals(cs2)`. This would mean manually checking lengths and iterating over characters or using a content comparison utility.

Recommended methods to fix:
org.apache.commons.lang3.StringUtils.equals(java.lang.CharSequence,java.lang.CharSequence)

